# kiosk-linux/configs/kernel-fz55.fragment
# Real-hardware enablement for the Panasonic Toughbook FZ-55 deployment
# target (first physical target hardware, selected 2026-07-24). Everything is
# built in (=y, no module infrastructure in this image) and purely ADDITIVE to
# the QEMU/virtio fragments — the same image boots under both QEMU (virtio
# devices) and the FZ-55 (Intel devices); each driver simply finds no device
# on the other platform.
#
# FZ-55 (Mk1/Mk2) hardware map this covers:
# - Intel UHD 620 graphics (Whiskey/Comet Lake iGPU) → i915 KMS driver;
#   userspace 3D comes from Mesa's iris Gallium driver (defconfig).
# - Intel gigabit ethernet → e1000e.
# - NVMe SSD and/or SATA (2.5" bay) → nvme + ahci + sd_mod.
# - USB 3.x xHCI ports (also how the OS image itself boots from a USB stick,
#   and keyboard/touchscreen input for the kiosk) → xhci + usb-storage + HID.
CONFIG_DRM_I915=y
CONFIG_DRM_I915_USERPTR=y
# CORRECTION (2026-07-25 hardware audit): this previously claimed i915 needs no
# firmware blobs. That holds for the Mk1's UHD 620 (Whiskey Lake), where bare
# KMS works — but NOT for Mk2 (Iris Xe / Tiger Lake) or Mk3 (Iris Xe / Raptor
# Lake), which need DMC firmware for display power management. Missing it
# degrades or removes display power states: flicker, no low-power display
# entry, and worse battery life on a terminal expected to last a shift.
# BR2_PACKAGE_LINUX_FIRMWARE_I915 in configs/desktop.fragment supplies the
# i915/ blob directory (adlp_dmc* for Raptor Lake's Alder Lake-P display IP,
# tgl_dmc* for Mk2). Firmware is loaded opportunistically, so the same image
# still boots fine under QEMU where no Intel GPU exists.

# Networking (wired). Wi-Fi (all 3 generations use an Intel combo card) is
# covered separately by kernel-wifi.fragment + kernel-bluetooth.fragment —
# this comment used to say "no Wi-Fi stack yet"; that stopped being true once
# those fragments were added and the comment was never updated. Fixed here
# rather than left contradicting the actual config.
CONFIG_E1000E=y

# Storage: NVMe + AHCI/SATA + the SCSI-disk layer both sit on.
CONFIG_BLK_DEV_NVME=y
CONFIG_ATA=y
CONFIG_SATA_AHCI=y
CONFIG_SCSI=y
CONFIG_BLK_DEV_SD=y

# USB: xHCI host controller (all FZ-55 ports), mass storage (the install USB
# stick this image is dd'd onto), and HID input (kiosk touchscreen/keyboard).
CONFIG_USB_SUPPORT=y
CONFIG_USB=y
CONFIG_USB_XHCI_HCD=y
CONFIG_USB_STORAGE=y
CONFIG_USB_HID=y
CONFIG_HID_GENERIC=y
CONFIG_INPUT_EVDEV=y
# FZ-55 touchscreen models expose HID multitouch devices.
CONFIG_HID_MULTITOUCH=y

# VFAT/EFI-adjacent bits: the boot partition tooling stays ext2/syslinux
# (legacy/CSM boot), but USB sticks reformatted in the field are commonly
# FAT — cheap to read support.
CONFIG_VFAT_FS=y
CONFIG_NLS_CODEPAGE_437=y
CONFIG_NLS_ISO8859_1=y

# ── Findings from the real Mk3 datasheet (fetched 2026-07-25 from
# ap.connect.panasonic.com), cross-checked against Mk1/Mk2 listings — every
# symbol below verified present in the pinned 6.6.63 kernel source before
# being added; none of this was guessed. ──────────────────────────────────

# Webcam: Mk1/Mk2/Mk3 all ship a UVC-class camera (Mk3: "2MP (1080p) w/
# privacy cover — Infrared with Windows Hello support"). RMPG Flex has its own
# field-camera capture workflow; without this the internal webcam is
# invisible to the OS entirely, not merely low quality.
# MEDIA_SUPPORT is a `menuconfig` gate, off by default in a minimal config —
# without it USB_VIDEO_CLASS cannot even be selected, let alone compiled.
# The gate chain here is deeper than it looks and cost a build cycle to find:
# MEDIA_SUPPORT alone is NOT enough. Setting only MEDIA_SUPPORT caused
# MEDIA_SUPPORT_FILTER to auto-enable (it defaults y when !EXPERT), which HIDES
# all camera drivers unless the specific category is also selected — so
# USB_VIDEO_CLASS silently vanished from the generated .config with no error.
# The full chain is MEDIA_SUPPORT -> MEDIA_CAMERA_SUPPORT (the category
# filter) -> MEDIA_USB_SUPPORT (the `if USB && MEDIA_SUPPORT` menuconfig
# guarding drivers/media/usb) -> VIDEO_DEV (V4L2 core) -> USB_VIDEO_CLASS.
CONFIG_MEDIA_SUPPORT=y
CONFIG_MEDIA_CAMERA_SUPPORT=y
CONFIG_MEDIA_USB_SUPPORT=y
CONFIG_VIDEO_DEV=y
CONFIG_USB_VIDEO_CLASS=y
CONFIG_USB_VIDEO_CLASS_INPUT_EVDEV=y

# MicroSDXC card reader (Mk3 spec: "MicroSDXC UHS-I"; present on Mk1/Mk2 too).
CONFIG_MMC=y
CONFIG_MMC_BLOCK=y
CONFIG_MMC_SDHCI=y
CONFIG_MMC_SDHCI_PCI=y

# I2C + touch/pen digitizer. The Mk3 FHD panel is "capacitive touch (1000
# nit)" with "4 models (glove, touch, pen, pen/touch)" and a stylus — a
# genuinely different input path from the plain HID multitouch already
# enabled above. Intel's mobile platforms expose their I2C-attached touch
# controllers through the "Designware" I2C core (LPSS), not a discrete PCI
# device, so that specific I2C host driver is required, not just generic I2C.
CONFIG_I2C=y
CONFIG_I2C_DESIGNWARE_CORE=y
CONFIG_I2C_DESIGNWARE_PLATFORM=y
# The Designware symbols above bring up the I2C *bus*; they do not attach
# anything to it. A capacitive touchscreen on an Intel mobile platform is a
# HID-over-I2C device enumerated from an ACPI node, which needs the I2C-HID
# transport driver on top of that bus before HID_MULTITOUCH (below) ever sees
# a report descriptor. Without it the controller is powered and addressable
# and still produces no input events — the failure looks like a dead
# touchscreen, not a missing driver, which is what makes it easy to miss.
# I2C_HID_ACPI selects the hidden I2C_HID_CORE symbol, so that one is not set
# here.
CONFIG_I2C_HID_ACPI=y

# CORRECTION (2026-07-25, second audit pass): the block above is still not
# sufficient, and neither half of it survives into the kernel .config on its own.
# Two separate reasons, both silent:
#
# (a) I2C_HID_ACPI lives inside `if I2C_HID`, and I2C_HID is a `menuconfig` gate.
#     Setting I2C_HID_ACPI without it is a no-op. CONFIG_I2C_HID=y is asserted
#     further down, in the "symbols this build depends on" block.
# (b) Reading the BUILT config rather than this fragment showed
#     I2C_DESIGNWARE_PLATFORM silently absent — it
# `depends on (ACPI && COMMON_CLK) || !ACPI`, and with ACPI=y but COMMON_CLK
# unset on x86_64 that dependency is unmet, so kconfig drops it without a word.
# The touchscreen this fragment claims to enable therefore had NO transport at
# all: the exact "blocker #1" the requirements doc named, still open, while the
# config looked like it addressed it.
#
# The full chain, bottom-up, is:
#   MFD_INTEL_LPSS_{PCI,ACPI} — the LPSS controllers the I2C host lives behind.
#     These `select COMMON_CLK` themselves, which is what makes
#     I2C_DESIGNWARE_PLATFORM selectable at all. Both PCI and ACPI enumeration
#     paths are enabled because which one a given generation uses is not
#     verifiable without the hardware.
#   PINCTRL_INTEL + the per-generation pinctrl driver — an I2C-HID digitizer is
#     declared in ACPI with a GpioInt interrupt. Without the matching pinctrl
#     driver that GPIO cannot be resolved, so the device never probes even with
#     a working I2C bus. One driver per FZ-55 generation:
#       CANNONLAKE -> Mk1 (Whiskey Lake-U)
#       TIGERLAKE  -> Mk2 (Tiger Lake-UP3)
#       ALDERLAKE  -> Mk3 (Raptor Lake-P shares Alder Lake pinctrl)
#     METEORLAKE is included as cheap forward cover for the Toughbook 56
#     successor named in the FZ-55 mk3 EOL notice.
# Corroborated by Panasonic shipping an "Intel Serial IO Driver" as a distinct
# factory package — that is this same LPSS block on the Windows side.
CONFIG_MFD_INTEL_LPSS=y
CONFIG_MFD_INTEL_LPSS_PCI=y
CONFIG_MFD_INTEL_LPSS_ACPI=y
CONFIG_COMMON_CLK=y
# CONFIG_PINCTRL is the `menuconfig` gate over drivers/pinctrl. Setting only the
# per-platform drivers below leaves them silently absent from .config — the same
# gate trap this project has now hit four separate times (MEDIA_SUPPORT for the
# webcam, SOUND/SND for audio, I2C_HID above, and this). Caught here by the
# post-build assertion in build.sh rather than by a dead touchscreen on a bench
# unit. PINCTRL_INTEL itself has no prompt and is `select`ed by the per-platform
# drivers; it is listed anyway so the assertion has a stable symbol to check.
CONFIG_PINCTRL=y
CONFIG_PINCTRL_INTEL=y
CONFIG_PINCTRL_CANNONLAKE=y
CONFIG_PINCTRL_TIGERLAKE=y
CONFIG_PINCTRL_ALDERLAKE=y
CONFIG_PINCTRL_METEORLAKE=y
# Wacom-class digitizer for the pen/stylus models. HID_MULTITOUCH (above)
# covers the capacitive touch layer; a Wacom-protocol pen is a separate HID
# usage this driver is needed to decode.
CONFIG_HID_WACOM=y

# USB4/Thunderbolt (Mk3 confirmed: "USB-C Thunderbolt 4, 40Gbps"; Mk2 listings
# also show a Thunderbolt 4 USB-C port). Without this, a Thunderbolt-only dock
# or accessory plugged into that port is invisible — plain USB devices on the
# same port still work via xHCI's USB fallback mode, so this specifically
# affects Thunderbolt-mode docks and DisplayPort-alt-mode external monitors.
CONFIG_USB4=y

# Serial-over-USB for the optional dedicated GPS module (Mk3: "Optional
# dedicated GPS (u-blox NEO-M8N)"). u-blox GPS modules on this class of
# hardware expose themselves as a USB CDC-ACM serial port (/dev/ttyACM0)
# carrying NMEA/UBX sentences; RMPG Flex has its own GPS ingestion pipeline
# that needs a real serial device to read from. Also generically useful for
# the optional WWAN modem's AT-command port (see below).
CONFIG_USB_ACM=y

# Optional WWAN (Mk3: "Optional 4G — 4G (LTE, LTE-A)"; Dual SIM Nano-SIM +
# eSIM). Kernel-side only — this enables the network/serial device nodes a
# WWAN modem needs to appear at all (as a QMI or MBIM network interface, or a
# plain AT-command serial port), but does NOT include a userspace modem
# manager (ModemManager). Units without the WWAN xPAK populated simply never
# see these drivers match anything, at zero cost. Units WITH it get basic
# connectivity; full cellular management (APN config, signal UI, SIM PIN)
# would be separate, larger scoped work if RMPG's purchased units actually
# have this option enabled.
CONFIG_USB_SERIAL=y
CONFIG_USB_SERIAL_OPTION=y
CONFIG_USB_NET_DRIVERS=y
CONFIG_USB_USBNET=y
CONFIG_USB_NET_QMI_WWAN=y
CONFIG_USB_NET_CDC_MBIM=y

# ── Security (Mk3 datasheet: "Secured-core PC", "TPM v.2.0", "NIST BIOS
# compliant") ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TPM 2.0. Modern "Secured-core PC" platforms (Mk3, and likely Mk2 given its
# listing also shows "TPM 2.0") expose the TPM over the UEFI-standard CRB
# (Command Response Buffer) interface rather than the older TIS interface —
# both are enabled since which one a given firmware/BIOS revision uses is not
# something verifiable without the physical unit, and enabling both costs
# nothing.
CONFIG_TCG_TPM=y
CONFIG_TCG_TIS=y
CONFIG_TCG_TIS_CORE=y
CONFIG_TCG_CRB=y

# ── Power (dual hot-swap batteries, Mk3 datasheet: two 10.8 V / 6,500 mAh
# packs, hot-swappable) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# These are `default y` in mainline's drivers/acpi/Kconfig and so are very
# likely already inherited from the BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_DEFCONFIG="x86_64" base.
# They are pinned here anyway because live code now depends on them and the
# dependency is invisible: rmpg-update's safe_to_update() reads
# /sys/class/power_supply/BAT*/capacity, and that glob is provided by
# ACPI_BATTERY alone. If a future defconfig bump or a kernel-version change
# ever drops the inherited default, the guard does not error — the glob simply
# fails to expand, the loop body never runs, and safe_to_update() returns
# success. It fails OPEN: every terminal in the fleet would take an update and
# reboot on a flat battery, which is the exact outcome the guard exists to
# prevent. A silent fail-open in a vehicle is worth three lines of config.
#
# ACPI_AC provides the mains-present state the same guard reads as
# BAT*/status. POWER_SUPPLY is `select`ed by both, and is stated explicitly
# for the same reason kernel-wifi.fragment states its crypto primitives.
CONFIG_ACPI_BATTERY=y
CONFIG_ACPI_AC=y
CONFIG_POWER_SUPPLY=y

# IOMMU (Intel VT-d). Part of what "Secured-core PC" means on the Windows
# side (Kernel DMA Protection), and independently good practice here because
# Thunderbolt (enabled above) is a DMA-capable external bus — an IOMMU is the
# standard mitigation against a malicious Thunderbolt device reading memory it
# should not be able to reach.
CONFIG_INTEL_IOMMU=y
CONFIG_INTEL_IOMMU_DEFAULT_ON=y

# ── Derived from Panasonic's OWN pre-installed component inventory ────────
# (2026-07-25, second pass.) Everything above this line was inferred from spec
# sheets. This block instead comes from the authoritative list Panasonic
# publishes of what it factory-installs on the Mk3 — retrieved for real from
# https://global-pc-support.connect.panasonic.com/driver/dr250205 (the earlier
# research session could not reach it; its network egress was blocked at the
# proxy, so its hardware map was search-summary guesswork).
#
# The reasoning is one-directional and worth stating: a driver on Panasonic's
# factory list means the silicon is PRESENT on at least some SKUs. It does not
# mean Linux needs the same driver. Each entry below names the Panasonic list
# item it answers, so a future reader can re-derive it rather than trust it.
#
# Every symbol verified to exist in the pinned 6.6.63 source and to have its
# dependencies already met in the merged .config — none guessed.

# Panasonic item 9: "Intel HID Event Filter driver (HID EFD)". This is the
# ACPI interface the FZ-55's function keys, volume keys and rotation lock
# arrive on. PANASONIC_LAPTOP (already =y via the base x86_64 defconfig) is the
# Let's Note interface and is NOT confirmed to bind on a Toughbook; intel_hid
# is the one Panasonic's own inventory proves is in use. Enabling both costs
# nothing and only one will bind.
CONFIG_INTEL_HID_EVENT=y

# Panasonic item 27: "Intel(R) Dynamic Tuning Technology (Intel(R) DTT)".
# DTT/DPTF is the platform's thermal policy engine. Base ACPI thermal (already
# on) only exposes the coarse critical-shutdown trip point; the INT340X driver
# is what exposes the real passive-throttling policy the firmware ships. On a
# terminal that lives on a vehicle dash in a Salt Lake City summer, the
# difference is throttling versus a thermal shutdown mid-shift.
# Selects ACPI_THERMAL_REL, ACPI_FAN, THERMAL_ACPI, INTEL_SOC_DTS_IOSF_CORE
# and INTEL_TCC on its own — no need to list them.
CONFIG_INT340X_THERMAL=y

# Panasonic item 8: "Intel Management Engine Software". The ME is present on
# every one of these platforms. mei/mei_me is also the transport several other
# subsystems ride on, and its absence makes some firmware-side features simply
# not enumerate.
CONFIG_INTEL_MEI=y
CONFIG_INTEL_MEI_ME=y

# Panasonic item 25: "Intel Integrated Sensor Solution" (gated on camera SKUs).
# The ISH is the low-power sensor hub carrying the ambient-light sensor. Only
# useful once something consumes it (auto-brightness is not built yet), but
# without it the sensors are invisible, so this is the cheap half of that
# future work.
CONFIG_INTEL_ISH_HID=y

# ── Networking: THREE distinct NICs, not one ─────────────────────────────
# The earlier doc guessed "e1000e, Intel I219-LM class" and flagged it as the
# single most likely first-article failure. Panasonic's list settles it and
# shows the guess was incomplete rather than wrong:
#   item 14 "LAN Driver for I219"          -> e1000e   (already =y, CONFIRMED)
#   item 23 "PCIe LAN(Realtek) 8111/8168"  -> r8169    (=y via base defconfig)
#   item 22 "USB LAN(Realtek) 8153/8152"   -> r8152    (ABSENT — added here)
# The USB one is not exotic: it is the Ethernet port on the dock and on every
# USB-C dongle, which is exactly how a docked in-vehicle terminal gets wired
# network. Without it a docked unit silently has no Ethernet.
CONFIG_USB_RTL8152=y
# I225/I226 is not on Panasonic's list for any FZ-55 generation, but it is the
# other Intel mobile NIC of this era and costs a few KB. Keeping it removes an
# entire class of "boots, no network" failure if a late SKU or the Toughbook 56
# successor swaps the controller.
CONFIG_IGC=y

# Panasonic items 19 + 20: two separate "USB Serial" driver packages. These
# back the serial port on the rear xPAK (FZ-VCN551W/552W/553W) and the
# barcode xPAK's virtual COM port (item 32). USB_SERIAL_OPTION above only
# covers cellular modems, not these bridges.
CONFIG_USB_SERIAL_FTDI_SIO=y
CONFIG_USB_SERIAL_PL2303=y
CONFIG_USB_SERIAL_CP210X=y

# OPAL self-encrypting drives. Many FZ-55 SKUs ship OPAL NVMe (and the
# Universal Bay 2nd-SSD xPAKs FZ-VSD55151W / FZ-VSD551T1W are explicitly OPAL).
# Without this the kernel can see a locked OPAL drive but cannot unlock or
# manage it, which presents as a disk that enumerates and then refuses every
# read — one of the confusing failure modes the requirements doc warns about.
CONFIG_BLK_SED_OPAL=y

# ── The menuconfig gate the I2C-HID block above needs ────────────────────
# I2C_HID is a `menuconfig`, so every symbol inside `if I2C_HID` — including
# I2C_HID_ACPI, set above — is unselectable until this is on. It came from the
# kernel's in-tree x86_64 defconfig by luck rather than by this project asking
# for it, which is a load-bearing accident for the one input device a terminal
# with no keyboard cannot do without. A kernel bump that changed the defconfig
# would have removed it silently, with no build error.
# (ACPI_BATTERY / ACPI_AC / POWER_SUPPLY are pinned for the same reason further
# up, by the battery-guard block.)
CONFIG_I2C_HID=y
